==English==
[[File:Latimeria chalumnae01.jpg|thumb|{{taxlink|Latimeria chalumnae|species}}]]

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|cœlacanth||obsolete}}

===Etymology===
From the {{bor|en|NL.|-}} genus name {{taxlink|Coelacanthus|genus}}, from {{der|en|grc|κοῖλος||hollow}} + {{m|grc|ἄκανθα||spine}}, referring to the hollow [[caudal fin]] rays of the first fossil specimen described and named by {{w|Louis Agassiz}} in 1839.<ref>{{R:Etymonline}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈsiː.lə.kænθ/}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-coelacanth.ogg|a=US}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# Either of two species of deep-water fish, {{taxlink|Latimeria chalumnae|species}} of the [[Indian Ocean]] and {{taxlink|Latimeria menadoensis|species}} of [[Indonesia]].
#: {{syn|en|gombessa}}
# Any [[lobe]]-[[finned]] fish in the order {{taxfmt|Coelacanthiformes|order}}, thought until 1938 to have been [[extinct]] for 70 million years.
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=2018|author=w:Tim Flannery|title=Europe: A Natural History|page=32|passage=Among them were ancient relatives of the piranhas and those popular aquarium fish the tetras, along with garfish and freshwater '''coelacanths''', known as mawsonids.}}

